.TH HTTP_CANREAD 3
.SH NAME
http_canread \- handle a readable socket of an HTTP client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <http.h>
.P
.BI "ssize_t http_canread(http* " h ", void (*" wantread ")(fd_type), void (*" wantwrite ")(fd_type));"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR http_canread ()
is called when the event loop reports that the socket of
.I h
can be read. With TLS it first completes the handshake and sends the request, as
.BR http_canwrite (3)
does. It then reads from the socket into the input buffer and, as long as
.I h->response->status
is HTTP_RECV_HEADER, parses the status line (into
.IR code )
and the headers: Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding: chunked and a multipart Content-Type select the
.I transfer
of the response, every line is appended to
.I data
and given to the
.I header
callback if there is one. The empty line ends the headers, the status becomes HTTP_RECV_DATA and
.I headers_len
holds the length of the header block in
.IR data .
.P
What happens to the body depends on
.IR h->stream .
When it is set the bytes stay in the input buffer, to be taken out by
.BR http_recv (3).
When it is not, they are appended to
.IR response->data .
.SH RETURN VALUE
The number of bytes that are in the input buffer, 0 when the peer closed the connection (the status is HTTP_STATUS_CLOSED), -1 on an error with h->err set; EAGAIN and EWOULDBLOCK only mean that there is nothing more for now.
.SH NOTES
Without h->stream the first byte of the body is copied into response->data together with the end of the headers, and http_read(3) then misses it. Use h->stream and http_recv(3).
.SH SEE ALSO
http(3), http_recv(3), http_canwrite(3)
